; worldterrain.eval - terrain kinds and landing cells the world can still be
; opened with (code-review batch 89: C345, C343).
;
; RUN IT THROUGH tools\WorldTest.py (phase 23), NOT ON ITS OWN. It WRITES to
; the project - terrain.cat on every terrain edit, world.map on `saveworld`, a
; glyph change and a delete - and worldreopen.eval is the other half: a SECOND
; launch that must open what this one left. Every fault here used to pass in
; the session that made it and abort the next launch in WorldMap::Load, which
; is why the reopen is the check that matters.
logecho on
reset
worldedit on

; --- C343: a doorway lands on a whole cell or none --------------------------
; gate2 is new and has no landing cell, so setting one half of it is HALF AN
; ENTRY: it used to leave entryz -1, and the save that wrote it aborted on its
; own read-back. crypt_gate lands on 7,8: one half still moves on its own
; there, but never below zero.
worldloc add dungeon gate2 12 6
worldloc set gate2 dungeon crypt
worldloc set gate2 level crypt1
expect-refuse worldloc set gate2 entryx 5
expect-refuse worldloc set crypt_gate entryz -2
expect-refuse worldloc set gate2 entry 4 -1
worldloc set gate2 entry 4 5
worldloc set gate2 entryx 6
worldloc
; the settings dialog's landing fields meet the same rule (its status row
; says so - a field edit, not a command, so it prints rather than refuses)
worldmap on
worldsettings crypt_gate
worldsettings entry 7 -3
worldsettings entry 7 7
worldsettings off
worldmap off
saveworld

; --- C345: two new terrains, a glyph each ------------------------------------
; A new kind used to get NO glyph, read as '?', so the second shared the
; first's and the next launch aborted on the clash.
newtype terrain
newtype terrain
world
; a glyph the world could not be read with is refused, and nothing is written:
; road's, a lowercase letter, two characters, none at all (the next `world`
; still shows terrain2's own glyph - a refusal that wrote first would have left
; it the '?' an empty or two-character glyph reads as)
expect-refuse typeset terrain terrain2 glyph -
expect-refuse typeset terrain terrain2 glyph b
expect-refuse typeset terrain terrain2 glyph CC
expect-refuse typeset terrain terrain2 glyph
; a new kind can be painted at once - the world was handed it - and the world
; is SAVED with it, so world.map now spells 6,6 with terrain2's glyph
terrainbrush terrain2
paint 6 6
saveworld
world
; a painted kind is refused a delete, and so is the road
expect-refuse typeset delete terrain terrain2
expect-refuse typeset delete terrain road
typerefs terrain terrain2
; a CHANGED glyph reaches the squares and world.map at once: on disk the
; square still read terrain2's OLD glyph, which terrain.cat no longer names
typeset terrain terrain2 glyph Q
world
; an undo takes back the paint and KEEPS today's kinds (the snapshot still
; held the old glyph), and the redo puts the paint back
undo
world
redo
world
; an unused kind deletes, and the kinds after it keep their squares
typeset delete terrain terrain1
world

; two more kinds for the world.map section below, made BEFORE the rename so
; they take terrain1 (a free id again) and terrain3, never the id the rename
; frees
newtype terrain
newtype terrain

; --- C345: a RENAME reaches the world's copy of the kinds -------------------
; terrain2 is painted on 6,6. The world syncs its copy of the kinds BY ID, so
; a rename that did not reach that copy left the brush refusing the new id and
; every later sync finding a painted kind gone - and a glyph changed after it
; never reached world.map. A second square under the new name, then a new
; glyph, which must reach both squares and the file.
typeset rename terrain terrain2 marsh
terrainbrush marsh
paint 7 6
typeset terrain marsh glyph R
world

; --- C345: world.map ON DISK, not the loaded world -------------------------
; A square painted and SAVED, then painted over in memory only: the loaded
; world holds none of the kind, but the file still does. A changed glyph and a
; delete must each write world.map - they were written only when the loaded
; world's text moved, which here it does not, and the next launch met a glyph
; terrain.cat no longer names. The glyph first: a delete's write first would
; have put the file right for both. And this section LAST, so nothing after it
; writes the world and hides a write it missed from the reopen.
terrainbrush terrain1
paint 8 6
terrainbrush terrain3
paint 9 6
saveworld
world
terrainbrush road
paint 8 6
paint 9 6
typeset terrain terrain3 glyph S
typeset delete terrain terrain1
world
validate
